Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Cap the work too: --max-evals

A termination check is not a fixed amount of work. Between two checks a
separation runs until it stops improving, so the same number of checks
took 1 s on one instance and 69 s on another. --max-evals counts the
sample evaluations the separator spends and stops the search once a
phase has spent its share, inside a separation if need be. It counts
work, not time, so it is as reproducible as --max-iterations, and a run
that never reaches it comes out exactly as without it.

